<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turku Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1280</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku">Turku</a> Castle is a medieval fortress at the mouth of the Aura River in Turku, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, begun around 1280 as a Swedish stronghold. For centuries it was the main seat of royal power in Finland. In the 1550s and 1560s Duke John, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-sweden">John III of Sweden</a>, held court there as ruler of the Duchy of Finland and enlarged it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style. It is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turku-castle.jpg" alt="Turku Castle" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otto Jula · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkucastle_edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Turku Castle stands on the banks of the Aura River and, with Turku Cathedral, is one of the oldest buildings still in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. It is the largest surviving medieval building in the country. The castle was the centre of the historical province of Finland Proper and the administrative centre of all Finland. It consists of the medieval keep, a square fort with two square gateway towers and walls 5 metres thick at the base, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> bailey. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> a moat joined it to the river, which made it basically an island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Swedes stationed in the region began the fort around 1280 as a military fortress and outpost. Over the next two centuries its defences were strengthened and living quarters were built, and it served as a defensive and administrative centre of Österland, the name of the region now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. The keep was finished in the early 15th century, and the bailey was begun in the late 15th century and finished in the 16th. Between 1395 and 1398 the castle may have served as a base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victual-brothers">Victual Brothers</a>, privateers turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, invited by its chief, Knut Bosson Grip. The castle also took part in power struggles within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> and stood sieges.</p><p>It reached its peak in the mid-16th century under Duke John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and Catherine Jagellon, who built the Renaissance Floor and the King's and Queen's halls. From 1573 to 1577 the deposed Swedish queen Karin Månsdotter was kept prisoner there. A great fire in 1614 destroyed the wooden structures of the old parts almost completely, and the main structure was largely abandoned and used as storage. The castle lost its status as administrative centre in the 17th century, after Per Brahe's period as governor-general ended. It was a prison from the 18th to the end of the 19th century, and the Russian Navy used it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-war">Finnish War</a> of 1808–1809.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Turku Historical Museum gained control of the castle in 1881, and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> took ownership after independence in 1917. Renovation began before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, stopped during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a>, when Soviet incendiary bombardment damaged the castle, and ended with a full restoration in 1987. The City of Turku took responsibility for it on 12 October 1993. It is a historical museum and part of the Turku Provincial Museum, and it is Finland's most visited museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Turku Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John set up his court at <b>Turku Castle</b>, which he enlarged in Renaissance style, and governed with considerable independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-finland">Duchy of Finland</a></li></ul></section>
